<Guide
  slug="local-call-transcription-windows"
  lede="Pick a speech engine that runs on your CPU, download its model once, and use it for both sides of the call. After that, turning speech into text needs no internet."
>
  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit. You don’t need a graphics card: these engines run on the CPU.</li>
    <li>100 to 470 MB for a model.</li>
    <li>Headphones, if you can. They give the cleanest result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choose a model</h2>
  <div class="guide-table" role="region" aria-label="Local speech models" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Download</th><th scope="col">Languages</th><th scope="col">When text appears</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3</th><td>465 MB</td><td>25 European languages</td><td>After each pause</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Parakeet CTC 110M</th><td>100 MB</td><td>English</td><td>After each pause</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sherpa-ONNX Zipformer</th><td>122 or 296 MB</td><td>English, or Chinese</td><td>While you speak</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">ORT Streaming Zipformer</th><td>122 or 296 MB</td><td>English</td><td>While you speak</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Start with Parakeet v3 if it covers your language. Choose a Zipformer if you want to see words as they are spoken.
    Whisper.cpp is in the app too, but only for recordings, not for live calls.
  </p>

  <h2>Set it up</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <a class="link" href="../../#download">Download NexQ</a> and run the installer. It installs for your user only and
      needs no admin rights. It isn’t code signed, so SmartScreen may warn you. You can also build it from source.
    </li>
    <li>Open <strong>Settings, STT Providers</strong>, choose Parakeet or Sherpa-ONNX, and download a model.</li>
    <li>
      Open <strong>Settings, Audio & Devices</strong> and pick a preset. <strong>Local Only (Parakeet)</strong> and
      <strong>Fully Offline</strong> (Sherpa-ONNX) put both sides on your PC.
    </li>
    <li>Check that You is your microphone and Them is the output your call plays through. Use <strong>Test</strong> on both.</li>
    <li>Press <kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">M</kbd> and join your call.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What “local” covers, and what it doesn’t</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The default isn’t local.</strong> A fresh install uses Web Speech for you and Deepgram for them, both
      online. The <strong>Free</strong> preset still sends your microphone to Web Speech.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Answers are separate.</strong> Local speech with an online AI still sends your transcript to that AI when
      you ask for an answer. To keep that local too, use
      <a class="link" href="../ollama-lm-studio-meeting-assistant/">Ollama or LM Studio</a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>It checks for updates.</strong> NexQ contacts GitHub for updates and release notes.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Files on disk aren’t encrypted.</strong> Transcripts and recordings are in
      <code>%APPDATA%\com.nexq.app</code>. API keys are in Windows Credential Manager.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check it yourself</h2>
  <p>
    After the model has downloaded, disconnect from the internet, start a meeting in NexQ, play a video file and talk over
    it. If both sides get text, nothing online was involved.
  </p>

  <h2>If it struggles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Text comes in bursts: Parakeet writes after a pause. Use a Zipformer for live words.</li>
    <li>It falls behind on an older PC: try a smaller model.</li>
    <li>
      Wrong words in your language: check that the model covers it. The
      <a class="link" href={doc('ai-providers')}>providers guide</a> lists each one. For another model, use
      <a class="link" href="../own-whisper-server/">your own Whisper server</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
